<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seuna Yadava dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1187–1317</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Yadavas of Devagiri, Seuna dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seuna Yadava dynasty ruled much of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from its capital Devagiri, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>, from the late 12th century. Former feudatories of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, Kakatiyas, Paramaras and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s raid on Devagiri in 1296 made them tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, which annexed the kingdom in 1317.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty.jpg" alt="Seuna Yadava dynasty" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yadavas_of_Devagiri._Bhillama_V._1185-1193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
